# Story fixtures fetched on demand (SQ-1015). NOT vendored — deliberately.
#
# `scripts/fetch-fixtures.sh` reads this file and populates
# `crates/app/tests/fixtures/stories/`, which `fixture_paths::fixture_path`
# already falls back to when a developer's gitignored `stories/` has no copy.
# CI runs it before `cargo test`; a developer with no `stories/` can run it too.
#
# WHY FETCH RATHER THAN COMMIT. The IF Archive's Terms of Use
# <https://ifarchive.org/misc/license.html> say it plainly: its contents "are the
# intellectual property of their original creators", distributed with permission
# where possible, and "Any material with no attached license is presumed to be
# licensed for personal use only." Downloading such a file is what the Archive
# exists for. REDISTRIBUTING it — which is what committing one to a repository
# that is itself published would be — is not covered, and no amount of "everyone
# calls it freeware" makes it covered. So the repository carries this manifest
# and never the bytes: the file arrives from the host that has permission to
# serve it, on every machine that wants it, and lanthorn hands it to nobody.
#
# The same reasoning is why commercial media stays out entirely. Fetching an
# Infocom floppy from a URL is the same act of copying as committing it with an
# extra hop; those suites keep skipping, and that is correct.
#
# LICENCE COLUMN. The strongest statement found for each file, checked one at a
# time rather than assumed from the directory it sits in:
#
#   gpl2-pd                      The author's own README: source under GNU GPL
#                                v2, card artwork public domain or GFDL.
#                                (scopa_z6/README, Aldo Cumani, February 2011)
#   cc-by-nc-nd-3.0              "Lost Pig by Admiral Jota and Grunk is licensed
#                                under a Creative Commons Attribution-
#                                NonCommercial-NoDerivs 3.0 Unported License."
#                                <https://grunk.org/lostpig/>
#   author-permission-ifarchive  "Brian Howarth gave his permission to upload the
#                                games to the IF Archive." (ReadMe.txt inside
#                                scott-adams/games/scottfree/mysterious_blorb.zip)
#                                Permission to THAT HOST to serve them, which is
#                                exactly the permission a fetch relies on.
#   ifarchive-tou                No licence statement located from the author.
#                                The IF Archive's Terms of Use govern: presumed
#                                personal use, freely downloadable, not ours to
#                                redistribute. Fetched, never committed.
#
# Nothing here is `ifarchive-tou` by default or by laziness — each was looked for
# and not found, including several works universally described as freeware
# (Photopia, Anchorhead's 1998 release, Spider and Web, The Wizard Sniffer, THE
# BAT). "IFDB says License: Freeware" is a cataloguer's metadata, not the
# author's grant, and is not recorded here as one.
#
# NOT FETCHED, and why — see docs/internals/ci-fixture-coverage.md:
#   fmvpoker.z6           the story is freely copyable by its author's own words,
#                         but its pictures are Zork Zero's (`fmvpoker.blb` is
#                         byte-identical to Infocom's `ZorkZero.blb`) and the
#                         author says so: "the graphics file is the intellectual
#                         property of Activision". Half the title is not a
#                         fixture — `v6_fmvpoker_hybrid` asserts render paths
#                         that only exist once the art is there.
#   Anchorhead.gblorb     the 2018 Special Edition is a PAID product
#                         (mikegentry5.itch.io/anchorhead). The 1998 z8 that IS
#                         fetched, `anchor.z8`, is a different work.
#   CounterfeitMonkey-11  the Archive carries release 12 and releases 5-9; the
#                         suites pin 11, which is on no upstream.
#   Alias 'The Magpie',   ditto: the local copies are releases the Archive no
#   frankenfingers        longer carries, so no digest can be pinned.
#
# FORMAT — one TAB-separated record per line:
#   sha256 <TAB> bytes <TAB> destination <TAB> url <TAB> zip-member-or-"-" <TAB> licence
# Destinations and zip members may contain spaces; nothing may contain a tab.
# The `bytes` column is redundant against the digest and kept anyway: it makes a
# truncated download say so before the hash does, and it is what a human reads.
